WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:07.360
Your goal is simple: eliminate all disease. The constraint is also simple: resources are scarce.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:14.660
You have limited money, time, and talent. Every dollar on a low-impact trial is a dollar not curing cancer.

3
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.020
Every researcher writing grant applications is a researcher not doing research.

4
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:26.480
Every patient excluded from trials is data you'll never collect and a person you'll never help.

5
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.560
Your current system fails this optimization so thoroughly it almost looks intentional.

6
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:41.900
Your N.I.H. spends forty-seven billion dollars a year. Your scientists spend 50 to 67 percent of their time writing grant

7
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:48.200
applications instead of doing science. Billions flow to projects that never produce treatments.

8
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.820
Your system isn't designed to maximize cures. It's designed to maximize grant-writing.

9
00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:01.780
This isn't a conspiracy. It's just what happens when you design a system that rewards asking for money instead of producing

10
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:09.890
results. You've accidentally built a machine whose primary output is paperwork and whose secondary output is occasionally,

11
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:19.640
reluctantly, medicine. Your Decentralized Institute of Health (D.I.H.) is an alternative design that optimizes for a single

12
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:27.960
metric: maximum R.O.I. toward disease eradication. It's what you'd build if you started from scratch and actually wanted

13
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.720
results. The Health-Industrial Complex: Coordinating Your War on Disease.

14
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:42.560
The Olsonian Problem. Your economist Mancur Olson identified why public goods are systematically underproduced: diffuse

15
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:50.860
benefits and concentrated costs. In simpler terms: everyone benefits from a cure for cancer, but nobody benefits enough

16
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.560
to fight for it the way a weapons manufacturer fights for a bomber contract.

17
00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:03.260
It's one of those observations that's obvious once someone says it, and invisible before, which describes most of your species'

18
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.500
problems. Curing cancer benefits 8 billion people a little.

19
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.300
Blocking cancer cures benefits a few thousand pharmaceutical executives a lot.

20
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:22.180
The executives show up to lobby. The 8 billion don't. This is why your species has spent 50 years "fighting cancer" while

21
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.360
your defense industry got stealth bombers, aircraft carriers, and G.P.S.

22
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.860
Your military-industrial complex solved this problem for defense.

23
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:37.840
Defense contractors, generals, politicians, and workers all have concentrated interests in military spending.

24
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:45.700
They coordinate. They lobby. They win budgets. Result: the most powerful military in human history.

25
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.600
Disease has no such coalition. Your patients are too sick to lobby.

26
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.420
Your researchers compete for scraps. Your funders lack coordination.

27
00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:04.040
Your politicians get no credit for cures that arrive after their term ends (which is all of them, because cures take 15

28
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:12.180
years and terms last 4). Everyone wants disease eradicated; no one has a concentrated interest in making it happen.

29
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:20.220
It's like everyone wanting a clean kitchen but nobody wanting to do the dishes, except the dishes are cancer and the kitchen

30
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:28.240
is on fire. Your D.I.H. solves the Olsonian problem by creating concentrated interests in disease eradication.

31
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:35.440
See The Incentive Stack for how. Your military-industrial complex coordinates actors around defense.

32
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:40.960
Your D.I.H. creates a health-industrial complex that coordinates actors around eradication.

33
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.150
Same structure, opposite purpose. SHAEF for Your War on Disease.

34
00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.960
In 1944, Eisenhower didn't replace the Allied armies. He coordinated them.

35
00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.400
Set the objective, allocated resources, made sure everyone pulled in the same direction.

36
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:05.440
Your D.I.H. is SHAEF for the war on disease. Pharma companies stay pharma companies.

37
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:10.020
Universities stay universities. Patient groups stay patient groups.

38
00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:16.320
But they operate as one force because your D.I.H. coordinates them toward eradication.

39
00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:22.540
Your war on disease has been losing for 50 years because it's not actually a war.

40
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:30.220
It's a collection of uncoordinated skirmishes where researchers compete for grants, pharma companies hide failures, and

41
00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:39.240
patients can't access trials. You've been "fighting" cancer the way a cat "fights" a laser pointer: lots of energy, no coordination,

42
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:46.580
nothing caught. Imagine if D-Day had been run this way. The Americans land at one beach, the British at another.

43
00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:53.880
Neither tells the other what they learned. Both compete for the same supply ships while the enemy reads their grant applications.

44
00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:01.500
Your D.I.H. is the coordination layer that turns scattered skirmishes into a unified campaign.

45
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.900
Your D.I.H.: The R.O.I. Maximization Protocol. Your D.I.H. is not a platform.

46
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:17.220
It's not an organization. It's not even really a thing. It's a coordination protocol with one function: "Make every actor

47
00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:21.360
do the highest-R.O.I. thing toward total disease eradication."

48
00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:28.860
This means: Every dollar flows to maximum impact. Every researcher works on highest-value problems.

49
00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:35.060
Every patient joins trials that matter most. Nothing gets wasted on low-R.O.I.

50
00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:41.240
activities. On your planet, you call this radical. On mine, we call it "obvious.".

51
00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:51.040
Three Core Functions. Your D.I.H. does exactly three things: Receive funds (from the one percent Treaty, donations, etcetera).

52
00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:57.560
Allocate research via patient subsidies (market mechanism) and infrastructure via Wishocracy.

53
00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:04.680
Verify results and pay proportional to impact. Everything operational is outsourced.

54
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:10.300
Trial infrastructure? That's your D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.)'s job.

55
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:14.560
Task decomposition? A.I. services. Talent matching? Existing marketplaces.

56
00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:21.900
Crowdfunding? Existing platforms. Why stay thin? Because thin protocols are hard to capture.

57
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.060
There's nothing to bribe. No operational role to corrupt. No C.E.O.

58
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.540
to take on a yacht trip. Just code that moves money toward measured outcomes.

59
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.010
Your species has a habit of corrupting every institution you build.

60
00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.120
You corrupt them the way water corrodes pipes: inevitably, given enough time and contact.

61
00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:48.800
This one is boring enough that nobody bothers. The Core Innovation: Pay for Results.

62
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:57.600
Every dollar flows based on results, not promises: Patients vote with their enrollment, which means researchers get paid

63
00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:06.540
for attracting patients. Outcomes determine continued funding, so campaigns that deliver get more, and failures get defunded.

64
00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:11.600
No one gets paid for writing grant proposals. No one gets paid for attending review committees.

65
00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.800
No one gets paid for publishing papers about why their research might work someday.

66
00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:24.900
This is how most other industries work. You pay contractors when they build the house, not when they promise to build it.

67
00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.040
You pay farmers when they grow the food, not when they apply for a farming license.

68
00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:35.380
Your Data Commons: Publish Everything. Your current system hides failures.

69
00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.720
Companies bury negative results. Researchers don't publish what didn't work.

70
00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:48.420
Your scientists waste billions repeating mistakes someone else already made because they literally cannot find out those

71
00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:54.720
mistakes were already made. It's like your whole species has amnesia, but only for the embarrassing parts.

72
00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.160
You remember your triumphs in high definition and forget your failures completely.

73
00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.770
This is called "being human." It's also called "why you keep dying.".

74
00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:14.800
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health, or D.I.H., requires one hundred percent open publication of all data, positive

75
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:22.100
and negative, as a condition of funding: Every trial, every result, and every dataset is published.

76
00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:27.480
A.I. models scan the global data commons, finding patterns humans miss.

77
00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.799
Failed experiments become shared knowledge, not repeated waste.

78
00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:40.840
This is intelligence sharing in the SHAEF analogy. Your Allies won partly because they shared Ultra intercepts across commands.

79
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.200
Your war on disease loses because everyone guards their failures like trade secrets.

80
00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.060
Think of it as a group chat where everyone shares what didn't work.

81
00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:56.320
Except the group is your entire species and the topic is death prevention.

82
00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:07.520
Governance: Market Mechanism and Wishocracy. Your D.I.H. uses two allocation mechanisms: Patient subsidies (market mechanism):

83
00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:15.440
Research funding follows patient choice. Patients enroll in trials they believe in; funding flows accordingly.

84
00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:23.930
Variable subsidy rates ensure rare diseases remain viable. No committees decide "cancer versus Alzheimer's."

85
00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:32.740
Wishocracy is for infrastructure and public goods only. It aggregates preferences through pairwise comparisons ("electronic

86
00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.060
health record, or E.H.R., integration or security audits?").

87
00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:41.480
The Incentive Stack: Making return on investment, or R.O.I., the Selfish Choice.

88
00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.420
Your D.I.H. doesn't rely on altruism. Altruism is lovely but flaky.

89
00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:52.780
It shows up when it feels like it, cancels last minute, and is always "busy that weekend."

90
00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.920
Instead, your D.I.H. pays everyone to do the highest R.O.I.

91
00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:04.760
thing. Greed is more dependable than kindness. Your entire economic system proves this daily.

92
00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:11.340
Nobody ever forgot to be greedy. For researchers, their self-interest is money and prestige.

93
00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:18.980
Their D.I.H. incentive is per-patient subsidies (higher for rare diseases), and their R.O.I.

94
00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:26.640
alignment is: attract patients equals get paid. For patients, their self-interest is health and compensation.

95
00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:32.200
Their D.I.H. incentive is that subsidies scale with trial importance, and their R.O.I.

96
00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.460
alignment is: joining high-priority trials equals more paid.

97
00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:42.280
For politicians, their self-interest is re-election and legacy.

98
00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:51.340
Their D.I.H. incentive is incentive alignment bonds, or I.A.B.s, tied to disease outcomes, and their R.O.I.

99
00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:59.790
alignment is: better outcomes equals a bigger bonus. For funders, their self-interest is impact and returns.

100
00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:05.180
Their D.I.H. incentive is quadratic matching and outcome tracking, and their R.O.I.

101
00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.780
alignment is: high R.O.I. donations equals amplified impact.

102
00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:15.280
For data providers, their self-interest is revenue. Their D.I.H.

103
00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:22.980
incentive is fees tied to data utility, and their R.O.I. alignment is: more useful data equals more revenue.

104
00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:28.260
For campaigns, their self-interest is funding. Their D.I.H.

105
00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:32.500
incentive is results-based continued funding, and their R.O.I.

106
00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:39.600
alignment is: higher R.O.I. equals more funding. Every incentive is R.O.I.-weighted.

107
00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:45.280
Not just "do good" but "do the MOST good per dollar or hour."

108
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.500
Your D.I.H. makes the highest R.O.I. action the selfish choice for every actor.

109
00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:58.540
Selfishness, properly directed, cures cancer. How Your Researchers Get Paid.

110
00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:03.900
Your traditional system: Write a grant proposal. Hope a committee likes it.

111
00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:09.180
Get paid to try. Maybe produce results. Maybe not. Get paid either way.

112
00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:17.300
Your D.I.H. system: Per-patient subsidies: The more patients who join your trial, the more funding you get.

113
00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:25.020
Patients vote with their enrollment. Variable subsidy rates: Rare diseases get higher per-patient subsidies.

114
00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:33.860
Small patient pools don't mean small budgets. Results-based continuation: Deliver results, get more funding.

115
00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:40.580
Don't deliver, get defunded. Like every other job on your planet except, somehow, research.

116
00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:47.780
Pay your scientists like you pay your plumbers: for fixing the problem, not for explaining why it's hard.

117
00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:54.360
How It Works For Your Patients. Your D.I.H. doesn't replace your healthcare system.

118
00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:58.480
It adds an "experimental treatment insurance layer" on top.

119
00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:06.440
Your species already understands insurance. You just hadn't thought to apply it to not dying of untreated diseases.

120
00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:12.560
Sarah has Type Two Diabetes. Old system: Metformin not working.

121
00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:16.800
No other options covered. Pays five hundred dollars a month for branded drug.

122
00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:24.260
Still has poor control. With your D.I.H.: Her doctor checks the trial network during a regular visit.

123
00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:29.020
Sees five relevant trials. Recommends one based on Sarah's profile.

124
00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.160
Sarah enrolls with one click. The trial coverage pool covers all costs.

125
00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:39.800
Sarah pays a thirty dollar copay. Gets an experimental drug that might work better.

126
00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:44.020
Reports blood sugar via app. Her data helps the next patient.

127
00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:51.720
Total time added to doctor visit: three minutes. Cost per patient per month: D.I.H.

128
00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:59.860
trial coverage costs between three hundred ten and two thousand five hundred ten dollars (medication monitoring data collection).

129
00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:06.960
A traditional trial costs between six thousand eight hundred and thirteen thousand six hundred dollars.

130
00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:15.820
The efficiency gain is seventy-five to eighty percent. Why your doctors will cooperate: There are more treatment options

131
00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:21.560
for desperate patients. Doctors receive continuing medical education credits, or C.M.E.

132
00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:26.480
credits, for participation. There is no liability (covered by trial insurance).

133
00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:31.500
It takes only one click in their electronic medical records, or E.M.R.s.

134
00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:40.400
There are no new systems to learn. Why your patients will use it: Affordable access to experimental treatments (twenty to

135
00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:45.880
fifty dollar copay). Doctor-recommended. Insurance-like coverage (familiar model).

136
00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:50.300
No financial risk. They help others while helping themselves.

137
00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:55.040
It's like being a guinea pig, except the guinea pig gets paid and gets medicine.

138
00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:03.880
How Your Politicians Get Aligned. Your politicians optimize for reelection, status, and post-office careers.

139
00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:08.660
"Humans continuing to exist" doesn't appear on their performance review.

140
00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:13.440
This is a design flaw in your democracy, not a moral failing.

141
00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:21.720
Well, it's both. But mostly it's a design flaw, because if you put saints in a badly designed system, you get the same results.

142
00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:31.340
You just feel more betrayed. Incentive Alignment Bonds solve this by making "support pragmatic clinical trial funding" the

143
00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:37.550
career-optimal move: Public Good Scores track voting records on health policy.

144
00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:42.080
Electoral support flows to high-scorers via independent PACs.

145
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:49.040
Post-office opportunities (fellowships, boards) reserved for leaders who governed well.

146
00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:58.720
No bribes. No corruption. Just a standing rule: support policies that measurably reduce suffering and your political career

147
00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:04.600
gets easier. Oppose them and it gets harder. Your politicians will figure it out.

148
00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:12.620
They're not stupid. They got elected, which requires a very specific and impressive type of intelligence, none of which

149
00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:18.200
involves curing diseases. How the Money Flows. The Architecture.

150
00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:24.100
The one percent Treaty Fund: Holds the treasury (from the one percent Treaty).

151
00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:28.500
Allocates between infrastructure and public goods via Wishocracy.

152
00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:35.870
Funds campaigns, not bureaucracies. No C.E.O., no board, no one to corrupt.

153
00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:42.660
Your Decentralized Institute of Health (D.I.H.): A thin coordination protocol.

154
00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:51.080
Receives funding FROM the one percent Treaty Fund. Research allocation via patient subsidies (market mechanism).

155
00:16:51.160 --> 00:17:02.660
Verifies results, pays for outcomes. Your D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.): A funded CAMPAIGN, not part of your D.I.H.

156
00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:09.260
itself. Provides technical infrastructure for trials. Competes with other research models for funding.

157
00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:14.540
Has no budget authority (just a service provider). The Fund Flow.

158
00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:23.980
Your one percent Treaty redirects twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year from global military budgets into the one

159
00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:32.540
percent Treaty Fund. But not all of it reaches research (some of it goes to keeping the machine running): Ten percent, or

160
00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:39.580
two point seven two billion dollars, goes to VICTORY Bond investors to repay campaign funders.

161
00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:49.140
Ten percent, or two point seven two billion dollars, goes to political incentives (Incentive Alignment Bonds, or I.A.B.s)

162
00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:57.900
to keep politicians aligned. Eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, goes to the Research treasury for

163
00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:05.120
patient subsidies (market allocation). What Gets Funded: Market Failures Only.

164
00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:10.600
Most research allocation happens automatically (patients choose trials, funding flows there).

165
00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:15.740
Your one percent Treaty Fund primarily funds MARKET FAILURES.

166
00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:18.920
These are the things your ecosystem can't handle on its own.

167
00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:25.280
Infrastructure Development and operations. Competing alternative implementations.

168
00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:32.080
Data commons infrastructure (storage, processing). Security audits and fraud detection systems.

169
00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:38.760
True Public Goods (No Revenue Model) Patient trial participation subsidies.

170
00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:45.120
Negative results publishing. Replication studies. This is minimal by design.

171
00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:53.440
Your ecosystem handles most research funding automatically: companies register treatments, patients join trials, revenue

172
00:18:53.440 --> 00:19:02.520
flows, research happens. Your D.I.H. only directs the one percent Treaty Fund to cover what the market genuinely can't.

173
00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:09.620
What Your D.I.H. Outsources (and Why). Your D.I.H. is intentionally minimal.

174
00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:18.280
It outsources everything operational because the best way to avoid corruption is to have nothing worth corrupting: Crowdfunding

175
00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:24.000
is outsourced to Gitcoin, Juicebox, and others because they already exist and work.

176
00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:30.320
Task decomposition is outsourced to A.I. services because machines are better at this.

177
00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:36.340
Talent matching is outsourced to existing marketplaces so you don't reinvent LinkedIn.

178
00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:45.320
Trial infrastructure is outsourced to D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.) to separate concerns.

179
00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:49.940
Data storage is outsourced to competing providers to encourage market competition.

180
00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:57.100
How Campaigns Plug In. Your D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.) is the main example of a funded campaign.

181
00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:01.860
It's not part of your D.I.H. It's a service provider competing for funding.

182
00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:08.620
If it stops performing, you fund something better. This is how your species runs restaurants.

183
00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:17.620
Time to try it with medicine. Campaign Lifecycle. Proposal: submit the campaign description, budget, and milestones.

184
00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:22.880
Wishocracy vote: humanity decides priority relative to alternatives.

185
00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:28.270
Funding: the treasury allocates based on the vote plus Optimocracy recommendations.

186
00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:33.960
Execution: the campaign delivers services (trials, infrastructure, and others).

187
00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:41.600
Verification: measure outcomes against milestones. Continuation: results determine next year's funding.

188
00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:46.900
Anti-Capture Design. Your current system is trivially captured.

189
00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:52.680
Concentrate billions of dollars in a few committees, and lobbyists will find them.

190
00:20:53.340 --> 00:21:01.140
This is as predictable as gravity, and your species keeps being surprised by it the way a dog is surprised by a vacuum cleaner.

191
00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:06.940
Every single time. Your D.I.H. makes capture economically irrational.

192
00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:13.820
How Your D.I.H. Resists Capture. Mechanism: No CEO. Nothing to bribe.

193
00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:18.060
Capture cost is not applicable. Mechanism: Algorithmic governance.

194
00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:23.040
Rules in smart contracts. You can't bribe an if-statement. Mechanism: Public ledger.

195
00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:27.280
Every dollar tracked. Corruption is visible. Mechanism: Forkable.

